ences (dashed black lines) and generated sequences (solid colored lines). g , 768 Global amino acid frequencies of real and generated sequences across 6 model organisms. 769\n770\n771\n772\n773\n774\n775\n776\n777\n\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\nFig. 4: Controllable generation of structured non-coding RNAs and protein-coding sequences\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled **a-h**, showing analyses of generated biological macromolecular/RNA sequence or structure outputs compared with natural/reference data.\n\nVisible content:\n\n- **Panel a:** Scatter/strip plot of **gRNA-miRNA-seq score**, grouped by categories including **Exact match**, **wobble pairing**, **isoacceptor**, **Incorrect isotype**, and likely **No detection**. Horizontal reference lines are shown. Points are color-coded by category.\n- **Panel b:** Stacked bar chart showing **percentage (%)** outcomes across amino-acid/tRNA-like labels. Legend categories include **Exact match**, **Wobble pairing**, **Isoacceptor**, **Incorrect isotype**, and **No detection**.\n- **Panel c:** Histogram/density plot of **sequence length (%)**, comparing **Class II (all tRNAs)** vs **Class I (tRNAs)** or similar class labels.\n- **Panel d:** Violin plots comparing **Real** vs **Generated** distributions for internal bit scores across RNA families: **5S**, **5.8S**, **16S**, and **28S**.\n- **Panel e:** Multiple scatter plots of **Isoelectric Point (pI)** vs **Molecular Weight (kDa)** for **natural sequences** and generated sequences under different **temperature / Top-p** sampling settings. Points are colored by **histone type**, with labels such as **H1**, **H2A**, **H2B**, **H3**, and **H4**.\n- **Panel f:** Boxplots of **ProGen2 perplexity** grouped by **histone type**, comparing natural sequences and generated outputs under several sampling conditions.\n- **Panel g:** Predicted protein/disordered-region visualization with labeled **N-term**, **C-term**, **Intrinsic Disordered Regions**, and **Globular Domain**. A confidence color scale labeled **pLDDT confidence** ranges roughly from low red/orange to high blue.\n- **Panel h:** Four circular/radial structural or sequence maps comparing **Natural Sequence** and **Generated** traces under different temperature/top-p sampling settings.\n\nBiological source objects visible or implied: **tRNAs or tRNA-like sequences**, **ribosomal RNA families 5S/5.8S/16S/28S**, and **histone proteins H1/H2A/H2B/H3/H4**.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces visible: comparison of **real/natural vs generated sequences**, generated under different **sampling parameters** such as **temperature** and **Top-p**.\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\nLikely findings shown visually: generated sequences are being evaluated against natural biological sequences by matching class, sequence length, covariance/internal bit score distributions, protein physicochemical properties, perplexity, predicted structure confidence, disorder regions, and circular structural/sequence representations.\na ,  Distribution  of  tRNAscan-SE  Cove  scores  for  generated  tRNAs,  categorized  by  anticodon  pairing behavior.  Solid  horizontal  lines  represent  medians. b ,  Proportion  of  anticodon  pairing  strategies spontaneously  adopted  by the  model  when  generating tRNAs for specific  amino  acids. c ,  Sequence length distribution for generated Class II (Leu/Ser) and Class I (others) tRNAs. d , Infernal bit scores for natural and generated ribosomal RNAs (5S, 5.8S, 18S, 28S). e , Physicochemical landscape (isoelectric available under a CC-BY 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.22.720063; this version posted April 24, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint point and molecular weight) of natural core histones and GenNA-generated histones across 778 incrementally increasing sampling temperatures and top-p thresholds. f , ProGen2 perplexity scores for 779 natural and generated sequences across five histone types. g , Schematic of the 3D spatial domains of a 780 histone, highlighting intrinsic disordered regions (IDRs). The structure is colored by AlphaFold2 pLDDT 781 confidence score (blue indicates high confidence; warm colors indicate low confidence). h , Radar charts 782 showing amino acid composition preferences of natural and generated histones modulated by different 783 sampling parameters. 784\n785\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled **a–h** describing a multimodal nucleotide sequence foundation model and tokenizer analysis.\n\nPanel **a** shows a workflow for **multi-modal nucleotide sequences**. Inputs include **NCBI RefSeq GenBank files** with plasmids/genomes/chromosomes, plus hybrid nucleotide language materials: **genomic DNA and RNA**, **natural language annotations**, and **XML structural tags**. These are processed by a **cross-modal BPE tokenizer** with **6,000 vocabulary size** and used to train a **GenNA Foundation Model**, described as a **Qwen3 decoder-only transformer with 3.6B parameters**. The model interface is shown for causal language modeling / next-token prediction over mixed biological and annotation tokens. Output capabilities include sequence-function alignment, understanding biological rules, zero-shot variant effect prediction, species-specific genomic fingerprints, latent-space clustering, phylogenetic tree reconstruction, unconditional self-guided generation, targeted ncRNA generation, and protein-coding sequence generation.\n\nPanel **b** is a pie chart showing source composition: **RNA 73.1%** and **genomic DNA 26.9%**.\n\nPanel **c** is a length distribution histogram comparing **genomic DNA** and **RNA** sequence lengths in characters. Both distributions are concentrated at shorter lengths with a long tail extending toward 20,000 characters.\n\nPanel **d** contains two taxonomic composition pie charts. Visible groups include **plant**, **invertebrate**, **vertebrate mammalian**, **vertebrate other**, **fungi**, and **protozoa**, with vertebrate other and plant comprising large portions.\n\nPanel **e** illustrates cross-modal BPE tokenization of a multimodal input sequence containing a tRNA annotation and nucleotide sequence, e.g. `...tRNA-Glu<seq>ATAC<gene><tRNA>TATTT...`. The sequence is transformed into a **BPE token sequence**, then mapped into **token IDs**.\n\nPanel **f** is a donut chart of tokenizer vocabulary composition for **vocabulary 6000**, broken down by token type and count. It includes nucleotide k-mers from **1-mer** through **9-mer and above**, plus **N-containing kmers** and **non-nucleotide** tokens. The largest visible segment is **7-mer**, followed by **8-mer** and **6-mer** categories.\n\nPanel **g** is a bar chart of **coverage by k-mer length**, showing decreasing coverage as k-mer length increases. Bars are labeled for k=1 through k=8 with percentages, including **100.0%** for 1-mers and lower coverage for longer k-mers.\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\nPanel **h** is a scatter plot comparing **character count** and **token count**, with a fitted line. The relationship is highly linear, with visible annotation approximately **Fit: y = 0.199x** and **r = 0.999**.\navailable under a CC-BY 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.22.720063; this version posted April 24, 2026. The copyright holder for this\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about a genomics or sequence model evaluated on gene symbols, nucleotide regions, mutations, molecule types, species, and phylogenetic structure.\n\nVisible panels:\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\n- **a**: Heatmap comparing “Real Gene Symbol” rows against “Provided Gene Symbol (Prompt)” columns. Gene symbols include ALKBH7, CPNE1, LYRM9, NAA16, PEX10, PNILPR1, RXFP3, SPSB3, TFAP2B. Colorbar is “Perplexity (Log Scale).” Diagonal entries are low/dark, off-diagonal entries are higher/yellow-green, indicating lower perplexity when the prompt gene symbol matches the real gene.\n- **b**: Boxplot of perplexity on log scale comparing “Matched” vs “Mismatched.” Mismatched prompts have much higher perplexity than matched prompts.\n- **c**: Triangular attention matrix labeled by token index query/key. Regions are annotated as 5' UTR, CDS, and 3' UTR, with an “Attention Weight” colorbar. Shows attention structure across sequence regions.\n- **d**: Boxplots of “Attention Ratio” by current generating region: 5' UTR, CDS, and 3' UTR. Legend “Attention On” includes Prompt, Tag, 5' UTR, CDS, and 3' UTR. Indicates attention is concentrated on different context regions depending on the region being generated.\n- **e**: Horizontal boxplots of Δ Perplexity (Mut - WT) for mutation classes: CDS Deletion, CDS Nonsense, CDS Stop-Loss, CDS Missense, CDS Synonymous, 3' UTR Substitution, 5' UTR Substitution, 3' UTR Deletion, 5' UTR Deletion. Coding-disruptive mutations show larger positive Δ perplexity.\n- **f**: Two mutation-position heatmaps. Rows are mutated bases A, C, G, T, and DEL. Columns are nucleotide positions. Top annotations mark 5' UTR, CDS, and 3' UTR. Colorbar is Δ Perplexity (Mut - WT). Deletions in CDS show strong positive effects; a zoomed heatmap highlights the start codon region.\n- **g**: Two t-SNE scatterplots. Left colored by molecule type, DNA vs RNA. Right colored by species groups: fungi, invertebrate, plant, protozoa, vertebrate mammalian, vertebrate other. Shows clustering by molecule type and taxonomic grouping.\n- **h**: Horizontal bar chart of perplexity by species/source object. Includes Saccharomyces cerevisiae, Arabidopsis thaliana, Drosophila melanogaster, Homo sapiens, and other species. Values vary substantially by species.\n- **i**: Heatmap of Δ Perplexity / penalty for true species label versus provided species label prompt. Species include Homo sapiens, Mus musculus, Gallus gallus, Danio rerio, Drosophila melanogaster, Caenorhabditis elegans, Arabidopsis thaliana, Oryza sativa Japonica Group, Chlamydomonas reinhardtii, and Saccharomyces cerevisiae.\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\n- **j**: Two phylogenetic/tree diagrams: “Tree based on PPL Penalty” and “Tree based on Ground Truth.” Compares model-derived sequence-distance structure with biological taxonomy.\nBiological source objects: nucleotide sequences with annotated 5' UTR, CDS/coding sequence, and 3' UTR regions; gene symbols; mutation classes; species labels across animals, plants, fungi, and other eukaryotes.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces shown: prompted gene-symbol and species-label conditioning, perplexity measurement, mutation perturbation Δ perplexity, attention maps/attention ratios, t-SNE embeddings, and tree reconstruction from perplexity penalty.\n\nMain visible findings: matched biological prompts reduce perplexity; coding-region disruptive mutations increase perplexity most; attention differs by generated sequence region; learned representations cluster by molecule type/species; perplexity penalties recover some taxonomic structure.\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological sequence generation figure.\n\nPanel **a**: Schematic workflow with three rows:\n- **Unconditional Generation**: prompt box `RNA,` leads to generated metadata such as “RNA, entelurus aequoreus, tripartite motif-containing protein 16 <seq>”, then to generated sequence containing tokens like `<seq><gene>` and nucleotide text.\n- **Species-specific Generation**: prompt `RNA, Homo sapiens,` leads to generated human metadata for `cdc42bpa, serine/threonine-protein kinase mrck alpha isoform 1 <seq>`, then generated sequence.\n- **Downstream Task Generation**: prompt `RNA, histone H1<seq>` directly leads to generated sequence with `<gene>`, `<cds>`, and nucleotide sequence tokens.\n\nPanels **b** and **c**: Scatterplots comparing generated sequences by:\n- x-axis: **BLAST Bit Score (Sequence Quality)**\n- y-axis: **Semantic Similarity**\n- marginal histograms on top and right\n- colored point clouds with trend curves\n- annotations:\n  - panel b: Spearman rho = 0.32, mean similarity = 0.79\n  - panel c: Spearman rho = 0.43, mean similarity = 0.70\n\nPanel **d**: Density plots comparing **Real Genome** and **Generated Sequences** distributions:\n- top: **CDS GC Content (%)**\n- bottom: **CDS GC3 Content (%)**\nGenerated sequences appear shifted toward higher GC and GC3 content than real genome distributions.\n\nPanel **e**: Boxplots comparing generated CDS nucleotide composition across species, with star markers for real genome values:\n- species: Human, Zebrafish, Fruit Fly, Arabidopsis, Yeast, Maize\n- top y-axis: **CDS GC Content (%)**\n- bottom y-axis: **CDS GC3 Content (%)**\n\nPanel **f**: Polar/radar-style codon usage plots for:\n- Human\n- Zebrafish\n- Fruit Fly\n- Arabidopsis\n- Yeast\n- Maize  \nEach compares real genome codon patterns against generated sequence patterns.\n\nPanel **g**: Amino acid frequency bar charts for the same six species:\n- Human, Zebrafish, Fruit Fly, Arabidopsis, Yeast, Maize\n- x-axis lists amino acid one-letter codes\n- y-axis: **Frequency**\n- legend compares **Generated** vs **Real Genome**\n\nVisible biological source objects include RNA sequences, coding sequences, genes, codons, amino acid frequencies, GC/GC3 content, and species-specific genomes from human, zebrafish, fruit fly, Arabidopsis, yeast, and maize.\n\nThe figure appears to evaluate a generative biological sequence model by comparing generated RNA/CDS outputs with real genomes using BLAST sequence quality, semantic similarity, nucleotide composition, codon usage, and amino acid frequency. A bioRxiv preprint watermark overlays the middle of the image.\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological/computational figure with panels labeled **a-h**.\n\nPanel descriptions:\n- **a**: Violin/scatter distributions of **NHAlignES score (bits)** for tRNA-related categories: **Exact match**, **Wobble pairing**, **Isoacceptor**, and **Incorrect isotype**. A horizontal reference line is shown around 70 bits.\n- **b**: Stacked bar chart by amino acid/isotype labels from **Gln** through **Phe**, showing percentages of categories: **Exact match**, **Wobble pairing**, **Isoacceptor**, **Incorrect isotype**, and **No detection**.\n- **c**: Histogram/density plot of **sequence length (nt)** comparing **Class II (Leu/Ser)** and **Class I (Others)**.\n- **d**: Violin plots comparing **Real** vs **Generated** sequences for internal bit scores across rRNA-like groups labeled **5S**, **5.8S**, **18S**, and **28S**.\n- **e**: Scatter plots of protein **Isoelectric Point (pI)** versus **Molecular Weight (kDa)**. Columns compare **Natural Sequences** and generated sequence settings: **Temperature 0.55 Top-p 0.7**, **Temperature 0.7 Top-p 0.8**, **Temperature 0.85 Top-p 0.9**, and **Temperature 1.0 Top-p 0.95**. Points are colored by histone type: **H1, H2A, H2B, H3, H4**.\n- **f**: Boxplots of **ProtGen2 Perplexity** across histone types **H1, H2A, H2B, H3, H4**, comparing natural sequences and generated sequences under the same temperature/top-p settings.\n- **g**: Protein structure visualization colored by **pLDDT confidence** scale from 40 to 100. Labels identify **N-term**, **C-term**, **Intrinsic Disordered Regions**, and **Globular Domain**.\n- **h**: Four polar/radar-style codon-frequency or codon-distribution plots comparing **Natural Sequence** with generated sequences under different temperature/top-p settings.\n\nBiological source objects visible:\n- tRNA/isotype categories and amino-acid labels.\n- rRNA categories: 5S, 5.8S, 18S, 28S.\n- Histone protein classes: H1, H2A, H2B, H3, H4.\n- Protein structural regions including disordered termini and globular domain.\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\nModel/interface elements:\n- Generated sequence settings controlled by **temperature** and **top-p** sampling.\n- Evaluation metrics include alignment/internal bit scores, sequence length, molecular weight, isoelectric point, ProtGen2 perplexity, pLDDT confidence, and codon distribution.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Exact matches generally show high NHAlignES scores compared with incorrect isotypes.\n- Generated rRNA-like sequences are compared directly against real sequences by internal bit score.\n- Generated histone-like sequences shift in molecular-weight/pI space as temperature/top-p increase.\n- Higher temperature/top-p settings appear to broaden generated sequence distributions.\n- Protein structure panel shows a compact globular domain with long low-confidence/disordered terminal regions.\n=== END ===\n\n=== NODE PATH:  (TaxonomyCodedDocument) ===\nInstance identifiers to fill (1 instances):\n[\n  {\n    \"title\": \"GenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\"\n  }\n]\n\n=== SCHEMA (fill all fields per instance) ===\n{\n  \"$defs\": {\n    \"TaxonomyCodedRoute\": {\n      \"description\": \"One route coded against taxonomy v1 while retaining lossless evidence.\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"route_label\": {\n          \"title\": \"Route Label\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"model_name\": {\n          \"title\": \"Model Name\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"lifecycle_phase\": {\n          \"enum\": [\n            \"pretraining\",\n            \"fine_tuning\",\n            \"inference\",\n            \"evaluation\",\n            \"unclear\"\n          ],\n          \"title\": \"Lifecycle Phase\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"task_or_configuration_verbatim\": {\n          \"title\": \"Task Or Configuration Verbatim\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"source_object_verbatim\": {\n          \"title\": \"Source Object Verbatim\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"source_object_normalized\": {\n          \"title\": \"Source Object Normalized\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"source_modality_normalized\": {\n          \"title\": \"Source Modality Normalized\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"tra