./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6/full_2026_07_06_rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6_docling_json_20260812_135517/docling/document.dclg:2022:      Figure 6. Low-resolution robustness on the Adipose Subcutaneous eQTL task. AUROC remains stable under moderate downsampling and degrades mainly at extreme resolutions.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000133_3abbdd0df15f/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000133_3abbdd0df15f_docling_json_20260812_220833/debug/dense_assembled_root.json:281:      "task_or_configuration_verbatim": "For the species-specific generation task, we selected six model eukaryotes as conditioning species. Without explicitly constraining gene function, we generated 2,000 RNA sequences for each species.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad/full_2026_07_06_rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad_docling_json_20260812_200246/docling/chunks.json:3367:    "text": "B DATASET STATISTICS AND DISTRIBUTIONS\nFigure 2 depicts the token length distribution of gene expression sequences post-tokenization with the Geneformer tokenizer, averaging 1843.2 tokens. The distribution peaks between 1000-1500 tokens, tapering off, with a spike at the 4096-token maximum, indicating some sequences are adjusted to this limit.\nFigure 3 illustrates the token length distribution of natural language descriptions tokenized with the Llama3.2-1B-Instruct tokenizer, with a mean length of 104.3 tokens. The distribution peaks around 100-150 tokens and decreases steadily, with fewer descriptions exceeding 200 tokens.\nFigure 4 shows a skewed distribution of cell types, with glial cells like oligodendrocytes dominating due to abundant brain-derived data, while immune cells like T and B cells are also prominent, reflecting bias toward easily accessible lymphoid tissues. Rarer types, such as stromal and plasma cells, are underrepresented, likely due to challenges in cell isolation and lower natural prevalence, highlighting how dataset composition reflects methodological biases rather than just biology.\nFigure 2: Token length distribution of gene expression sequences after tokenization with the Geneformer tokenizer.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a single-panel quantitative plot: a histogram titled “Length Distribution - combined.”\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis label: “Length”\n- Y-axis label: “Frequency”\n- Light blue histogram bars showing a length distribution.\n- Red dashed vertical reference line labeled in the legend as “Mean: 1843.2.”\n- The distribution spans roughly from low hundreds to just over 4000 length units.\n- There is a prominent high-frequency spike near the far right around length ~4000.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- No visible biological specimens, microscopy images, cells, tissues, organisms, molecules, or sequence logos are shown.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- No model architecture, image transformation pipeline, segmentation overlay, embedding plot, or interface diagram is visible.\n\nFinding shown:\n- The figure summarizes the combined frequency distribution of lengths, with a reported mean of 1843.2 and a large concentration at the maximum/right-end length range.\nFigure 3: Token length distribution of natural language descriptions after tokenization with the Llama-3.21B-Instruct tokenizer.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single-panel histogram showing the distribution of tokenized `natural_desc` lengths for a combined dataset. The x-axis is labeled `# tokens`, and the y-axis is labeled `Frequency`. The title reads `Tokenized natural_desc Length - combined`.\n\nThe histogram uses light green bars, with most observations concentrated roughly between 70 and 130 tokens and a right-skewed tail extending beyond 300 tokens. A red dashed vertical line marks the mean, with the legend indicating `Mean: 104.3`.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, molecular structures, model architecture diagrams, or transformation pipelines are visible.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad/full_2026_07_06_rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad_docling_json_20260812_200246/docling/chunks.json:3481:    "text": "B DATASET STATISTICS AND DISTRIBUTIONS\nFigure 4: Overview of the distribution of cell types in the dataset. For clarity, only the 30 most abundant categories out of 783 are shown.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel vertical bar chart showing counts by biological cell-type categories.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis label: `Categories`\n- Y-axis label: `Count`\n- Bars are sorted descending by count and colored with a blue-to-red gradient.\n- Numeric count labels are shown above each bar.\n- The highest categories include:\n  - `oligodendrocyte`: 29,725\n  - `neuron`: 27,981\n  - `CA4 pyramidal alpha neuron`: 21,994\n  - `T cell`: 20,957\n  - `fibroblast`: 20,220\n  - `glutamatergic neuron`: 19,635\n  - `CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell`: 18,755\n  - `B cell`: 18,206\n- Lower-count categories visible include immune, neuronal, stromal, and tissue-associated cell types such as `microglial cell`, `plasma cell`, `neutrophil`, and `stromal cell`.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Cell-type categories, including neural cells, immune cells, epithelial cells, fibroblasts, macrophages, monocytes, and stromal cells.\n\nTransformations / model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to summarize categorical frequency counts, likely from a biological dataset or cell annotation output.\n- No model architecture, embedding, microscopy image, or algorithmic interface is shown.\n\nFinding:\n- The distribution is imbalanced, with oligodendrocytes and neurons having the largest counts, while categories such as neutrophil and stromal cell have the smallest visible counts.\nFigure 5 shows a skewed distribution of the top 30 disease categories (out of 128), with normal (592,932) and COVID-19 (61,961) dominating. The high count for normal likely stems from extensive use of healthy control samples in research to establish baselines, while COVID-19's prominence reflects widespread data collection during the pandemic.\nFigure 6 shows a skewed distribution of the top 30 tissue categories (out of 347), with brain (211,063) and blood (177,460) leading, likely due to extensive sampling in neurological and hematological research. Tissues like lung (87,784) and eye (59,430) follow, reflecting biases toward accessible or clinically relevant sources.\nFigure 7 shows a skewed distribution of assay categories, with 10x v3 (480,361) and 10x v2 (242,493) dominating, likely due to their widespread adoption in droplet-based single-cell RNA sequencing. Assays like scRNA-seq3 (86,205) and 10x transcription profiling (53,623) follow, reflecting a bias toward scalable, standardized protocols.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad/full_2026_07_06_rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad_docling_json_20260812_200246/docling/chunks.json:3595:    "text": "B DATASET STATISTICS AND DISTRIBUTIONS\nFigure 6: Overview of the distribution of tissue (general) categories in the dataset. For clarity, only the 30 most abundant categories out of 347 are shown.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a single-panel quantitative bar chart titled `final_combined_tissue_general_top_values - Top 30 Categories (Log Scale)`.\n\nVisible content:\n- Panel type: vertical bar chart.\n- X-axis label: `Categories`.\n- Y-axis label: `Count (Log Scale)`.\n- Scale: logarithmic count scale.\n- Bars show the top 30 tissue/general biological categories by count.\n- Each bar has a numeric value label above it.\n\nBiological source objects / categories shown include:\n`brain`, `blood`, `lung`, `eye`, `breast`, `heart`, `kidney`, `liver`, `small intestine`, `skin of body`, `colon`, `bone marrow`, `placenta`, `endocrine gland`, `respiratory system`, `musculature`, `nose`, `pancreas`, `adipose tissue`, `reproductive system`, `intestine`, `stomach`, `digestive system`, `exocrine gland`, `lymph node`, `vasculature`, `prostate gland`, `uterus`, `mucosa`, `adrenal gland`.\n\nFindings visible from the chart:\n- `brain` has the highest count, labeled `211,063`.\n- `blood` is second, labeled `177,460`.\n- `lung` is third, labeled `87,784`.\n- Counts decline across categories, with the lowest visible category being `adrenal gland`, labeled `5,708`.\n- The chart uses a blue-to-red color gradient across ranked bars, but no legend or panel subdivision is visible.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad/full_2026_07_06_rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad_docling_json_20260812_200246/docling/chunks.json:3729:    "text": "B DATASET STATISTICS AND DISTRIBUTIONS\nFigure 8: Overview of the distribution of pathway categories in the dataset.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel bar chart showing counts by category.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis label: `Categories`\n- Y-axis label: `Count`\n- Bars are sorted in descending order from left to right.\n- Category labels are rotated diagonally and appear to be Hallmark gene set names from MSigDB.\n- The tallest labeled categories include:\n  - `HALLMARK_UV_RESPONSE_DN` with count `302,664`\n  - `HALLMARK_APICAL_JUNCTION` with count `205,134`\n  - `HALLMARK_ALLOGRAFT_REJECTION` with count `167,957`\n  - `HALLMARK_ANDROGEN_RESPONSE` with count `151,116`\n  - `HALLMARK_INTERFERON_ALPHA_RESPONSE` with count `147,365`\n  - `HALLMARK_HEDGEHOG_SIGNALING` with count `130,456`\n  - `HALLMARK_CHOLESTEROL_HOMEOSTASIS` with count `122,403`\n  - `HALLMARK_INTERFERON_GAMMA_RESPONSE` with count `115,216`\n  - `HALLMARK_TNFA_SIGNALING_VIA_NFKB` with count `107,099`\n- Additional visible categories include pathways such as `MYC_TARGETS_V2`, `PI3K_AKT_MTOR_SIGNALING`, `MYOGENESIS`, `KRAS_SIGNALING`, `APOPTOSIS`, `EPITHELIAL_MESENCHYMAL_TRANSITION`, `COAGULATION`, `HYPOXIA`, `MITOTIC_SPINDLE`, `HEME_METABOLISM`, `ADIPOGENESIS`, `PEROXISOME`, `OXIDATIVE_PHOSPHORYLATION`, and `KRAS_SIGNALING_DN`.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Gene-set/pathway categories, specifically Hallmark biological processes/signatures.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to visualize aggregated count values per Hallmark category.\n- No model architecture, microscopy, molecular structures, or computational workflow interface is shown.\n\nFinding:\n- `HALLMARK_UV_RESPONSE_DN` has the highest count, followed by `HALLMARK_APICAL_JUNCTION` and `HALLMARK_ALLOGRAFT_REJECTION`; counts decrease steadily across the remaining Hallmark categories.\nFigure 8 shows a skewed distribution, with hallmark TNFα via NF κ B (302,664) and hallmark UV response DN (205,134) leading, likely due to inflammation and stress studies, boosted by brain and blood tissue dominance (Figure 6). Pathways like hallmark interferonα response (167,957) and hallmark allograft rejection (151,116) follow, reflecting immune biases from lymphoid samples. Rare pathways like hallmark spermatogenesis (3,109) are underrepresented, possibly due to tissue specificity.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003008_6a0122710a41/full_2026_07_06_rec_003008_6a0122710a41_docling_json_20260812_200717/docling/document.md:96:In Extended Data Figure 4 and Extended Data Figure 5, we present examples of the ability of SFT-based and BFT-based LLMs to master gene knowledge and infer biological processes. In the first example, while SFT provides a generic overview, BFT demonstrates superior biological granularity by identifying specific mechanisms like MHC II presentation and SARS-CoV-2 spike activation. BFT also captures critical clinical insights missed by SFT, such as CTSL's regulation by cystatin and its status as a potential therapeutic target. For a more detailed comparison, please refer to Extended Data Table 1. In the second example, the answer of BFT is more accurate, as it considers the biological functions of the gene more comprehensively, especially in terms of genome stability and nuclear lamina organization. However, the answer of SFT is overly simplified in terms of tumor growth inhibition and ignores the key role of the gene in cell structure and genome stability. For a more detailed comparison, please refer to Extended Data Table 2.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003008_6a0122710a41/full_2026_07_06_rec_003008_6a0122710a41_docling_json_20260812_200717/docling/document.md:102:Considering that many downstream biological tasks can be accomplished through representation learning, we further examined whether the embeddings generated by BFT-based LLM capture biological knowledge. As shown in Extended Data Figure 6a, we obtained response texts from BFTbased LLM and generated their embeddings using Youtu-Embedding [17]. The UMAP visualization in Figure 2a shows that genes with similar biological functions form distinct clusters, indicating that BFT-based representations reflect biological heterogeneity at the gene level. Compared with scGPT [18] and GenePT [15], BFT-based embeddings exhibit more compact and biologically meaningful clustering. While scGPT relies on large-scale biological pretraining and GenePT adapts ChatGPT with high computational cost, BFT-based embeddings achieve strong biological meaning without requiring any domain-specific data management.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003008_6a0122710a41/full_2026_07_06_rec_003008_6a0122710a41_docling_json_20260812_200717/docling/document.md:132:We further evaluated the potential of BFT in cell-level embedding representation. As shown in Extended Data Figure 6b, we directly obtained cell embeddings by weighted aggregation of singlecell expression data and gene embeddings. In Figure 2d, we evaluated the heterogeneity of cell embeddings using phenotypic labels and cell type labels respectively, and found that BFT-based embeddings perform the best. This result also reveals an interesting phenomenon: BFT-based embeddings outperform scGPT, a single-cell foundation model pre-trained with large-scale singlecell datasets. This indicates that BFT indeed has the ability to align LLMs with biological knowledge. In Extended Data Figure 8, we used UMAP to compare the PCA embeddings of raw data and BFT-based embeddings. We found that BFT-based embeddings eliminate batch effects across patients and distinguishes clusters of different cell types.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003008_6a0122710a41/full_2026_07_06_rec_003008_6a0122710a41_docling_json_20260812_200717/docling/document.md:333:Extended Data Figure 4: This case examines LLMs' mastery of genetic knowledge. The black text is the user prompt, the blue text is SFT response, and the orange text is BFT response.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003008_6a0122710a41/full_2026_07_06_rec_003008_6a0122710a41_docling_json_20260812_200717/docling/document.md:359:Extended Data Figure 6: Workflow for extracting biological embeddings from LLM-BFT. a : LLMBFT generates responses based on entities of interest (e.g., a specific gene). The textual description of the gene is input into Tencent Youtu-Embedding to obtain gene embeddings. b : For a single-cell dataset, gene embeddings are weighted by gene expression values to generate cell embeddings.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_135854/docling/document.json:2837:      "orig": "To gain a deeper understanding of OmniNA's capabilities in aligning sequence and textual patterns with natural lan- guage, we investigated the token representations of the pretrained OmniNA-1.7B (see the 'Materials and methods' section). The t-SNE plot reveals the model's ability to discriminate tokens representing distinct semantics, such as genus, genes, and sequences (Fig. 3 A). Besides, tokens for gene symbols and gene functions exhibit distinct clustering, while sequence tokens with different base preferences manifest in separate loci (Fig. 3 A). Moreover, taxonomy names are distinctly separated, with tokens from the same taxonomy clustering together (Fig. 3 B). We also observed that viruses from different phyla form distinct subclusters (Fig. 3 B).",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_135854/docling/document.json:2838:      "text": "To gain a deeper understanding of OmniNA's capabilities in aligning sequence and textual patterns with natural lan- guage, we investigated the token representations of the pretrained OmniNA-1.7B (see the 'Materials and methods' section). The t-SNE plot reveals the model's ability to discriminate tokens representing distinct semantics, such as genus, genes, and sequences (Fig. 3 A). Besides, tokens for gene symbols and gene functions exhibit distinct clustering, while sequence tokens with different base preferences manifest in separate loci (Fig. 3 A). Moreover, taxonomy names are distinctly separated, with tokens from the same taxonomy clustering together (Fig. 3 B). We also observed that viruses from different phyla form distinct subclusters (Fig. 3 B)."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_135854/docling/document.json:2999:      "orig": "Figure 2. Evaluation of OmniNA's generation performance. ( A ) Fidelity assessment in sequence annotations generated by OmniNA models. For each target and model, the precision, recall, and F1 score represent the mean score across all labels. ( B ) Example of generated annotations for the same input by OmniNA models of varying sizes. ( C ) (Left) Bar plot illustrating Shannon entropy for taxonomy token generation. The heatmap at the bottom represents the generated token numbers. (Right) Line chart depicting generated token numbers correlated with token frequency. ( D ) (Left) Bar plot illustrating Shannon entrop y f or gene function token generation. The heatmap at the bottom represents the generated token numbers. (Right) Line chart depicting generated token numbers related to token frequency. ( E ) Box plot showcasing the cosine similarity of feature representations among different annotations generated from the same sentence. ( F ) t-SNE plot deciphering feature representations of generated annotations, with annotations from the same sequence colored identically. ( G ) B o x plot reflecting the cosine similarity of token embeddings among different annotations generated from different truncations of the same sequence. ( H ) t-SNE plot deciphering the feature representations of generated annotations, with annotations from the same genome colored identically. ∗∗∗ P -value < 0.001; ∗∗ P -value < 0.01; ∗ P -value < 0.05.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_135854/docling/document.json:3000:      "text": "Figure 2. Evaluation of OmniNA's generation performance. ( A ) Fidelity assessment in sequence annotations generated by OmniNA models. For each target and model, the precision, recall, and F1 score represent the mean score across all labels. ( B ) Example of generated annotations for the same input by OmniNA models of varying sizes. ( C ) (Left) Bar plot illustrating Shannon entropy for taxonomy token generation. The heatmap at the bottom represents the generated token numbers. (Right) Line chart depicting generated token numbers correlated with token frequency. ( D ) (Left) Bar plot illustrating Shannon entrop y f or gene function token generation. The heatmap at the bottom represents the generated token numbers. (Right) Line chart depicting generated token numbers related to token frequency. ( E ) Box plot showcasing the cosine similarity of feature representations among different annotations generated from the same sentence. ( F ) t-SNE plot deciphering feature representations of generated annotations, with annotations from the same sequence colored identically. ( G ) B o x plot reflecting the cosine similarity of token embeddings among different annotations generated from different truncations of the same sequence. ( H ) t-SNE plot deciphering the feature representations of generated annotations, with annotations from the same genome colored identically. ∗∗∗ P -value < 0.001; ∗∗ P -value < 0.01; ∗ P -value < 0.05."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_135854/docling/document.json:3408:      "orig": "Figure 4. Performance of fine-tuned OmniNA on nucleotide sequence identification tasks. ( A ) Radar plot shows the F1 score achieved by OmniNA and the benchmark method on the task of DNA element detection. Bar plot illustrating the F1 score achie v ed b y OmniNA model and the benchmark method on the task of splice site detection ( B ), PAS/TIS site detection ( C ), and gRNA off-target prediction ( D ). ( E ) Dot plot depicts the performance of OmniNA with different parameters on the task of methylated region detection. The circle size reflects the precision score of method, and the color reflects the recall of the method. The bar plot in the above panel shows the F1 score of the OmniNA model with different parameters. ( F ) The performance of OmniNA and three benchmark methods, i.e. Enformer, Sei, and ExPecto, on chromatin feature detection tasks. ( G ) The boxplot presents the median, upper, and lo w er quartiles of the F1 score for the pathogenic variation detection performance across all genes. ( H ) Radar plot shows the F1 score of the OmniNA-1.7B model and benchmark method for pathogenic SNV detection. ( I ) Violin and box plots comparing the o v erall F1 score distributions between EVE and OmniNA-1.7B. T -test was applied for the analysis. ( J ) Bar plot illustrating the F1 score achieved by OmniNA and the benchmark method on the task of pathogenic virus detection. Data are randomly sampled 1000 times from each branch, with 10 0 0 strains per sample. The center line represents the mean, and the error bar indicates the 95% CI. ( K ) Bar plot illustrating the F1 score achieved by OmniNA and the benchmark method on the task of bacteria species classification. Data are randomly sampled 1000 times from each branch, with 10 0 0 strains per sample. The center line represents the mean, and the error bar indicates the 95% CI. ( L ) The F1 score comparison between the OmniNA-1.7B model and benchmark method on bacteria phylum classification task. ∗∗∗ P -value < 0.001.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_135854/docling/document.json:3409:      "text": "Figure 4. Performance of fine-tuned OmniNA on nucleotide sequence identification tasks. ( A ) Radar plot shows the F1 score achieved by OmniNA and the benchmark method on the task of DNA element detection. Bar plot illustrating the F1 score achie v ed b y OmniNA model and the benchmark method on the task of splice site detection ( B ), PAS/TIS site detection ( C ), and gRNA off-target prediction ( D ). ( E ) Dot plot depicts the performance of OmniNA with different parameters on the task of methylated region detection. The circle size reflects the precision score of method, and the color reflects the recall of the method. The bar plot in the above panel shows the F1 score of the OmniNA model with different parameters. ( F ) The performance of OmniNA and three benchmark methods, i.e. Enformer, Sei, and ExPecto, on chromatin feature detection tasks. ( G ) The boxplot presents the median, upper, and lo w er quartiles of the F1 score for the pathogenic variation detection performance across all genes. ( H ) Radar plot shows the F1 score of the OmniNA-1.7B model and benchmark method for pathogenic SNV detection. ( I ) Violin and box plots comparing the o v erall F1 score distributions between EVE and OmniNA-1.7B. T -test was applied for the analysis. ( J ) Bar plot illustrating the F1 score achieved by OmniNA and the benchmark method on the task of pathogenic virus detection. Data are randomly sampled 1000 times from each branch, with 10 0 0 strains per sample. The center line represents the mean, and the error bar indicates the 95% CI. ( K ) Bar plot illustrating the F1 score achieved by OmniNA and the benchmark method on the task of bacteria species classification. Data are randomly sampled 1000 times from each branch, with 10 0 0 strains per sample. The center line represents the mean, and the error bar indicates the 95% CI. ( L ) The F1 score comparison between the OmniNA-1.7B model and benchmark method on bacteria phylum classification task. ∗∗∗ P -value < 0.001."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_135854/docling/document.json:6069:          "text": "SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled A-H comparing OmniNA language models on nucleotide-related tasks.\n\nVisible content:\n- Panel A: Performance table for three tasks: nucleotide sequence classification, taxonomy classification, and enzyme classification. Models compared are OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, and OmniNA-66M. Metrics shown are Precision, Recall, and F1, with blue/green heatmap shading.\n- Panel B: Example nucleotide input sequence and generated model responses. Shows a true response for an Amolops voucher KIZ014116 tyrosinase gene, partial CDS, and outputs from OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, and OmniNA-66M. Entity spans are color-highlighted, including organism names, voucher/accession-like identifiers, gene/protein terms, CDS, mRNA, and “uncharacterized protein.”\n- Panel C: Taxonomy token statistics. Bar chart reports Shannon entropy and token number for OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, and OmniNA-66M. Adjacent line plot shows token number across token-frequency bins.\n- Panel D: Gene function token statistics. Similar Shannon entropy/token number bar chart and token-frequency distribution line plot for the three OmniNA models.\n- Panel E: Boxplots of cosine similarity for OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, OmniNA-66M, and Random, with significance markers.\n- Panel F: Fit-SNE embedding plot showing clustered sequence/function representations. Annotated clusters include “mRNA of dehydrogenase,” “mRNA of oxidase,” “Chloroplast CDS/mRNA,” and “Subunit of NSL complex.” At right, an example nucleotide input and repeated OmniNA-1.7B responses list multiple species names paired with “KAT8 regulatory NSL complex subunit 2.”\n- Panel G: Boxplots of cosine similarity for HIV gene-related outputs from OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, OmniNA-66M, and Random, with significance markers.\n- Panel H: Fit-SNE embedding of HIV1 gene representations. Legend includes HIV1 genes: gag, gag-pol, vif, vpr, vpu, env, and nef. A schematic genome-like gene map is shown above/right, and model response examples list HIV-1 isolate descriptions with highlighted gene labels such as gag and pol.\n\nBiological source objects and concepts visible:\n- Nucleotide sequences.\n- Taxonomic labels/organism names.\n- Genes and gene products, including tyrosinase, KAT8 regulatory NSL complex subunit 2, HIV1 gag, gag-pol, vif, vpr, vpu, env, and nef.\n- Sequence annotation terms such as CDS, mRNA, partial CDS, and protein.\n\nModel/interface details:\n- Evaluates OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, and OmniNA-66M.\n- Shows prompt-like nucleotide “Input” strings and text “Response” outputs.\n- Uses highlighted spans to compare generated annotations against true responses.\n- Uses tokenization/statistical analyses, cosine similarity boxplots, and Fit-SNE embeddings to characterize model outputs and learned representations.\n\nMain visible findings:\n- Larger OmniNA models generally show higher classification scores and higher cosine similarity than smaller models and random baseline.\n- Token statistics differ by model size for taxonomy and gene function.\n- Embeddings cluster by biological annotation/function categories, including chloroplast annotations and HIV1 gene classes."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_135854/docling/document.json:6108:          "text": "SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled A-H comparing OmniNA language models on nucleotide-related tasks.\n\nVisible content:\n- Panel A: Performance table for three tasks: nucleotide sequence classification, taxonomy classification, and enzyme classification. Models compared are OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, and OmniNA-66M. Metrics shown are Precision, Recall, and F1, with blue/green heatmap shading.\n- Panel B: Example nucleotide input sequence and generated model responses. Shows a true response for an Amolops voucher KIZ014116 tyrosinase gene, partial CDS, and outputs from OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, and OmniNA-66M. Entity spans are color-highlighted, including organism names, voucher/accession-like identifiers, gene/protein terms, CDS, mRNA, and “uncharacterized protein.”\n- Panel C: Taxonomy token statistics. Bar chart reports Shannon entropy and token number for OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, and OmniNA-66M. Adjacent line plot shows token number across token-frequency bins.\n- Panel D: Gene function token statistics. Similar Shannon entropy/token number bar chart and token-frequency distribution line plot for the three OmniNA models.\n- Panel E: Boxplots of cosine similarity for OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, OmniNA-66M, and Random, with significance markers.\n- Panel F: Fit-SNE embedding plot showing clustered sequence/function representations. Annotated clusters include “mRNA of dehydrogenase,” “mRNA of oxidase,” “Chloroplast CDS/mRNA,” and “Subunit of NSL complex.” At right, an example nucleotide input and repeated OmniNA-1.7B responses list multiple species names paired with “KAT8 regulatory NSL complex subunit 2.”\n- Panel G: Boxplots of cosine similarity for HIV gene-related outputs from OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, OmniNA-66M, and Random, with significance markers.\n- Panel H: Fit-SNE embedding of HIV1 gene representations. Legend includes HIV1 genes: gag, gag-pol, vif, vpr, vpu, env, and nef. A schematic genome-like gene map is shown above/right, and model response examples list HIV-1 isolate descriptions with highlighted gene labels such as gag and pol.\n\nBiological source objects and concepts visible:\n- Nucleotide sequences.\n- Taxonomic labels/organism names.\n- Genes and gene products, including tyrosinase, KAT8 regulatory NSL complex subunit 2, HIV1 gag, gag-pol, vif, vpr, vpu, env, and nef.\n- Sequence annotation terms such as CDS, mRNA, partial CDS, and protein.\n\nModel/interface details:\n- Evaluates OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, and OmniNA-66M.\n- Shows prompt-like nucleotide “Input” strings and text “Response” outputs.\n- Uses highlighted spans to compare generated annotations against true responses.\n- Uses tokenization/statistical analyses, cosine similarity boxplots, and Fit-SNE embeddings to characterize model outputs and learned representations.\n\nMain visible findings:\n- Larger OmniNA models generally show higher classification scores and higher cosine similarity than smaller models and random baseline.\n- Token statistics differ by model size for taxonomy and gene function.\n- Embeddings cluster by biological annotation/function categories, including chloroplast annotations and HIV1 gene classes.",
