./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6/full_2026_07_06_rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6_docling_json_20260812_193428/docling_graph/graph.json:9648:      "supporting_figure_or_table": "Figure 6",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6/full_2026_07_06_rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6_docling_json_20260812_193428/docling_graph/provenance.json:2472:      "text": "4. Experiments\n4.5. Interpretability Analysis\nWe probe model interpretability with Grad-CAM on the multi-page fusion representations (see Appendix K.7 for implementation details). Fig. 4 visualizes two pages from a donor-labeled Japonica test document. Unlike sequencebased models that distribute attention diffusely at the base level, OpticalDNA produces localized attributions over short contiguous regions, reflecting region-level processing induced by visual tokenization and local receptive fields. This phrase-like reading behavior mirrors how humans process long text and is particularly well suited for long DNA documents, as it avoids exhaustive base-wise scanning while preserving informative local patterns under a fixed token budget. Importantly, Grad-CAM activations concentrate around the annotated donor splice site (purple box), and the corresponding page receives higher overall attribution, indicating that OpticalDNA can both localize biologically meaningful splice signals and prioritize the most relevant pages during inference. Overall, these results indicate that OpticalDNA learns structured, region-aware representations aligned with genomic reasoning, while also offering practical interpretability, highlighting its potential utility for large-scale genomic and transcriptomic analyses.\nTable 6. ROI identification on T2 (IoU = 0 . 99 ).\n\nOpticalDNA-HG38, LCM ↑ = 0.9871. OpticalDNA-HG38, Joint ↑ = 0.9820. OpticalDNA-HG38, Strict ↑ = 0.8763. OpticalDNA-HG38, ℓ ∞ ↓ = 0.3941. OpticalDNA-Rice, LCM ↑ = 0.9991. OpticalDNA-Rice, Joint ↑ = 0.9692. OpticalDNA-Rice, Strict ↑ = 0.8084. OpticalDNA-Rice, ℓ ∞ ↓ = 0.3243\nAbbrev. LCM: line-count match; Joint: line-level joint accuracy (text+ROI); Strict: page-level strict success.\nFigure 4. Grad-CAM visualization on multi-page fusion for a donor case (two pages). Purple boxes indicate donor splice sites; numbers denote page-level mean attribution.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows two side-by-side text panels containing dense nucleotide/DNA-like sequences highlighted in green. Each panel appears to be a page or sequence block comparison view.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Left panel labeled `Page#0: 0.530`\n- Right panel labeled `Page#4: 0.585`\n- A horizontal color/score bar at the bottom ranging from `0` to `1`, with midpoint `0.5`\n- Most sequence text is shaded green, suggesting a continuous heatmap or confidence/similarity overlay\n- A vertical divider separates the two panels\n\nBiological source objects:\n- DNA/RNA nucleotide strings composed primarily of A, C, G, and T characters\n\nLikely transformation/interface:\n- Sequence text has been rendered as page images and overlaid with a green intensity map, possibly representing similarity, attribution, relevance, or matching score.\n- The page-level numeric labels may indicate scores for each extracted page/panel.\n\nVisible finding:\n- Both panels contain strong green-highlighted sequence regions, with the right panel having a slightly higher displayed score (`0.585`) than the left (`0.530`).",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6/full_2026_07_06_rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6_docling_json_20260812_193428/docling_graph/provenance.json:2704:      "text": "4. Experiments\n4.7. Rendering Robustness\nFigure 6. Low-resolution robustness on the Adipose Subcutaneous eQTL task. AUROC remains stable under moderate downsampling and degrades mainly at extreme resolutions.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle line chart showing model performance across image downsampling resolutions.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: “Downsampled resolution” with labels `64×64`, `128×128`, `256×256`, `320×320`, `640×640`.\n- Y-axis: “AUROC”, ranging roughly from `0.76` to `0.84`.\n- Green line with circular markers connects AUROC values:\n  - `64×64`: `0.768`\n  - `128×128`: `0.799`\n  - `256×256`: `0.812`\n  - `320×320`: `0.828`\n  - `640×640`: `0.813`\n- Dashed horizontal reference line labeled “Native 640×640” near AUROC `0.812`.\n- Finding: AUROC improves from low resolution to `320×320`, peaks at `0.828`, then decreases slightly at `640×640` to `0.813`, close to the native-resolution reference line.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, anatomical structures, transformations, or model interface diagrams are visible in this extracted image.",
