./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6/full_2026_07_06_rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6_docling_json_20260812_193428/docling_graph/nodes.csv:169:"TaxonomyCodedRoute_1732df86cb450831","TaxonomyCodedRoute","entity","TaxonomyCodedRoute","Figure 6","OpticalDNA","evaluation","low-resolution robustness by downsampling the rendered 640 × 640 DNA images while keeping the DNA content and layout fixed","Adipose Subcutaneous eQTL task","Adipose Subcutaneous eQTL task","image","['downsampling the rendered 640 × 640 DNA images while keeping the DNA content and layout fixed']","['downsample rendered DNA images while preserving content and layout']","rendered 640 × 640 DNA images","visual_raster_carrier","raw_slide_or_patch_input","downsampling the rendered 640 × 640 DNA images while keeping the DNA content and layout fixed","unclear","no_text_on_this_route","ablation_only","We further evaluate low-resolution robustness by downsampling the rendered 640 × 640 DNA images","4.7. Rendering Robustness","Figure 6","text_plus_figure","","{""document_id"": ""4abd6ce3a2fe319a846033a3aa19a464"", ""match"": ""verbatim"", ""chunks"": [33], ""pages"": [9], ""refs"": [""#/texts/161"", ""#/pictures/5""], ""spans"": [{""chunk"": 33, ""start"": 41, ""end"": 49}]}","","","",""
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002517_46d135b6aad4/full_2026_07_06_rec_002517_46d135b6aad4_docling_json_20260812_213057/docling_graph/provenance.json:2346:      "text": "5 Further Analysis\nRicher vocabulary enhances single-cell interpretation and generation. To identify the optimal balance between vocabulary richness and computational efficiency, we conduct an ablation study on two cell generation tasks, as depicted in Figure 3. For random cell generation, we observe that vocabulary expansion consistently improves the generation of valid and ideally lengthed cell sentences, regardless of the extent of the increase. However, uniqueness remains largely unaffected by the expansion, consistently nearing 100%. For pseudocell generation, introducing 7,000 gene names initially leads to a performance dip, possibly as the partial vocabulary expansion introduces ambiguity. Performance improves as the vocabulary expands to 14,000 gene names and continues to rise until reaching optimal levels at a 21,000 gene name threshold. At this point, the model is equipped with an extensive set of single-cell terminologies, enabling it to accurately understand and handle the intricate relationships within single-cell data.\nVocabulary adaptation improves domainspecific language understanding. To further investigate vocabulary adaptation's effect, we use t-SNE to visualize general versus domain-specific word embeddings before and after training, shown in Figure 4. Initially, embeddings overlap significantly. After training, domain-specific words begin to form discrete clusters, evidenced by a lower Davies-Bouldin (DB) index, indicating better clustering. This implies that the embeddings of these words have become more representative of their specific meanings and contexts within their respective domains, rather than remaining ambiguously intermingled with general words. In contrast, general word embeddings remain largely unchanged, highlighting the precision of our vocabulary adaptation strategy in bolstering domain expertise without compromising general language processing.\nFigure 4: Visualization of word embeddings before and after training. A lower DB index indicates better clustering separation.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nTwo-panel scatter plot comparing word embeddings or token distributions before and after a transformation.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Legend at top: blue dots = “General words”; red dots = “Domain-specific words”.\n- Left panel labeled “Before” with red text “DB Index: 99.58”.\n- Right panel labeled “After” with red text “DB Index: 8.00”.\n- A right-pointing arrow between panels indicates a transformation from before to after.\n- Both panels show 2D scatter coordinates with blue points broadly distributed in a circular/cloud pattern and red points clustered near the center.\n- In the “After” panel, the red domain-specific cluster appears more vertically elongated and more separated/structured relative to the blue points than in the “Before” panel.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visibly shown. The figure concerns general vs domain-specific words, not biological specimens, cells, tissues, or molecular structures.\n\nModel/interface details:\n- No explicit model architecture or interface is shown.\n- The DB Index likely refers to a clustering/separation metric, but the figure does not define it.\n\nFinding shown:\n- The transformation reduces the displayed DB Index from 99.58 to 8.00, suggesting improved separation or clustering quality between general and domain-specific word groups.\nCHATCELL demonstrates robustness to novel phrasing. Given the diversity of human expression, maintaining robust performance across variously phrased instructions is essential for a chat model. Hence, we replace all task descriptions in the test set with variations that the model has not previously encountered during training or validation, to assess its proficiency with unseen instructions. Figure 5 illustrates the results for the drug sensitivity prediction. Despite the novel phrasing of instructions, the model's performance remains almost unchanged. This highlights the model's robustness and generalization ability, effectively handling tasks with varied descriptions and enhancing conversational single-cell analysis.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002517_46d135b6aad4/full_2026_07_06_rec_002517_46d135b6aad4_docling_json_20260812_213057/docling_graph/provenance.json:2422:      "text": "5 Further Analysis\nFigure 5: Performance (%) comparison of drug sensitivity prediction using task descriptions seen or unseen during training.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a scientific figure comparing model/task generalization for cancer cell drug sensitivity prediction.\n\nVisible content:\n- Two top text panels describe task descriptions:\n  - Left: “Task descriptions seen in training:” Predict whether a cancer cell is drug resistant or sensitive using its top 100 genes expressed in descending expression order.\n  - Right: “Task descriptions unseen in training:” Evaluate whether a cancer cell is resistant or sensitive to a drug by analyzing the descending order of its 100 highest expressed genes.\n- An arrow connects the seen-training task description to the unseen-training paraphrased task description.\n- Color legend:\n  - Pink: Seen task descriptions\n  - Light blue: Unseen task descriptions\n- Two grouped horizontal bar charts:\n  - Left chart labeled `GSE149383`\n  - Right chart labeled `GSE117872`\n- Metrics shown on the y-axes:\n  - Recall\n  - Precision\n  - F1\n  - Acc\n- The bars compare performance under seen vs unseen task descriptions.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Cancer cells\n- Drug resistance/sensitivity phenotype\n- Top 100 expressed genes ordered by expression level\n- Datasets labeled `GSE149383` and `GSE117872`\n\nModel/task interface:\n- The model receives a natural-language task description plus a “cell sentence” consisting of top expressed genes in descending expression order.\n- The output is binary classification: drug resistant vs sensitive.\n\nFindings shown:\n- Performance is very similar between seen and unseen task descriptions.\n- On `GSE149383`, all metrics are around 93.5-93.9.\n- On `GSE117872`, all metrics are around 96.1-97.0.\n- The figure suggests robustness to paraphrased or unseen task descriptions.\nCHATCELL accurately captures cell type characteristics in generation. To assess the biological accuracy of cell sentences generated by CHATCELL, we visualize their corresponding gene expression matrices in two dimensions, following the pseudo-cell generation experimental setting. Figure 6 (a) shows that cell distributions generated by CHATCELL closely resemble those of real cells, confirmed by Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD). The distinct clustering of cell types in the generated data demonstrates CHATCELL's ability to capture and differentiate unique cell characteristics, indicating its capacity to generate detailed profiles for each cell type.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002517_46d135b6aad4/full_2026_07_06_rec_002517_46d135b6aad4_docling_json_20260812_213057/docling_graph/provenance.json:2478:      "text": "5 Further Analysis\nCHATCELL uncovers gene-level biological insights. Each cell sentence is composed of genes. To delve into the biological significance of the representations learned at a finer granularity, we analyze gene associations based on their embeddings obtained by CHATCELL. We employ K -means clustering to categorize genes, grouping those with high associations, as depicted in Figure 6 (b). For each cluster, we further conduct gene pathway enrichment analysis (Aleksander et al., 2023). This analysis, which focuses on a series of interconnected biochemical reactions working together to perform specific biological functions like energy production or cell growth, offers insights into the biological processes each cluster is involved in. Specifically, we map these genes to their related pathways in the Gene Ontology (Ashburner et al., 2000) to identify the biological pathways signifi- cantly associated with them, with the significance of these associations quantified by p -values, as illustrated in Appendix Figure 8. As illustrated, genes in cluster 1 are linked to tissue structure regulation, while those in cluster 2 are predominantly related to the immune system, inflammatory responses, and cell signaling. Cluster 3 encompasses genes associated with immune cell activation and signaling, cluster 4 with leukocyte functions and immune responses, and cluster 5 primarily with tissue development. The distinct separation of these gene clusters further demonstrates CHATCELL's effective capture of intricate relationships between genes and their functions, validating its profound understanding of complex biological processes.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002517_46d135b6aad4/full_2026_07_06_rec_002517_46d135b6aad4_docling_json_20260812_213057/docling_graph/provenance.json:2535:      "text": "5 Further Analysis\nFigure 6: (a) Distribution of real cells and generated cells. (b) Gene clusters and their associated pathways.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure with panels labeled **(a)** and **(b)**.\n\nPanel **(a)** shows single-cell embedding plots comparing **real** and **generated** cells. The top plot is labeled **“Cell Source”** and overlays two categories: **Real** and **Generated**, with an annotation **“MMD: 0.007”**. Two lower plots are labeled **“Real Cells”** and **“Generated Cells”**. A legend lists cell-type labels including **Basal**, **Dermal Fibroblast**, **Dermal Papilla**, **Endothelial**, **Hair Shaft-cuticle.cortex**, **IRS**, **Infundibulum**, **Isthmus**, **Medulla**, **Mix**, **ORS**, **Spinous**, **TAC-1**, **TAC-2**, and bulge-related CD34+ populations. The visual comparison suggests the generated cell distribution closely matches the real cell distribution in the embedding space.\n\nPanel **(b)** shows a clustered cell embedding with clusters labeled **1–5** in a legend. The clusters are shown as colored point clouds arranged along a horizontal manifold. Text annotations associate regions with biological processes: **immune system, inflammatory response, cell signaling processes**, **leukocyte functions, immune responses**, **immune cell activation and signaling**, **regulation of tissue structure**, and **tissue development**. Small illustrative icons appear near these annotations. The panel appears to interpret cluster-level biological functions across the embedding.\n\nBiological source objects visible or referenced include cell populations from skin or hair follicle contexts, such as basal cells, dermal fibroblasts, dermal papilla, endothelial cells, hair shaft/cuticle/cortex, inner root sheath, infundibulum, isthmus, medulla, outer root sheath, spinous cells, transit-amplifying cells, and CD34+ bulge-associated cells.\n\nThe figure presents a model output comparison between real and generated single-cell data, likely using dimensionality reduction embeddings and a distributional similarity metric, with downstream cluster/function interpretation.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002517_46d135b6aad4/full_2026_07_06_rec_002517_46d135b6aad4_docling_json_20260812_213057/docling_graph/provenance.json:4671:      "text": "E Additional Results\nFigure 7 complements Figure 5 by displaying the model's robustness to seen and unseen task descriptions under both base battery-half and large battery-full configurations. This demonstrates CHATCELL's essential chat model capability to handle diverse expressions of task descriptions.\nFigure 7: Performance (%) comparison of drug sensitivity prediction using task descriptions seen or unseen during training under base (a) and large (b) settings.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart figure with panels labeled **(a)** and **(b)**.\n\nPanel structure:\n- **(a)** contains two horizontal bar charts:\n  - Left: dataset **GSE149383**\n  - Right: dataset **GSE117872**\n- **(b)** contains two horizontal bar charts:\n  - Left: dataset **GSE149383**\n  - Right: dataset **GSE117872**\n\nLegend:\n- Pink bars: **Seen task descriptions**\n- Blue bars: **Unseen task descriptions**\n\nMetrics shown on the y-axis:\n- **Recall**\n- **Precision**\n- **F1**\n- **Acc**\n\nVisible findings:\n- In panel **(a)**, GSE149383 scores are around **95.27–95.68**, with unseen task descriptions slightly higher than seen for several metrics.\n- In panel **(a)**, GSE117872 scores are around **97.71–98.49**, with seen and unseen values appearing identical or nearly identical for each metric.\n- In panel **(b)**, GSE149383 scores are around **97.09–97.83**, with seen task descriptions higher than unseen.\n- In panel **(b)**, GSE117872 scores are around **98.46–99.24**, with seen task descriptions higher than unseen.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- The only visible biological identifiers are dataset names **GSE149383** and **GSE117872**, which appear to be GEO accession IDs. No cells, tissues, genes, microscopy images, or molecular structures are visible.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure compares model performance under **seen vs unseen task descriptions** using classification metrics. No model architecture, pipeline, embedding, or interface diagram is shown.\nMeanwhile, Figure 8 provides a detailed expansion of Figure 6 (b), showing each gene cluster and its most associated pathways. This proves CHATCELL's ability to capture the relationships between genes and their functions, thereby enhancing its comprehension of biological processes.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6/full_2026_07_06_rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6_docling_json_20260812_193428/debug/dense_reconciliation.json:516:        "route_label": "Figure 6"
