./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad/full_2026_07_06_rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad_docling_json_20260812_200246/docling/document.json:6226:      "orig": "Figure 8 shows a skewed distribution, with hallmark TNFα via NF κ B (302,664) and hallmark UV response DN (205,134) leading, likely due to inflammation and stress studies, boosted by brain and blood tissue dominance (Figure 6). Pathways like hallmark interferonα response (167,957) and hallmark allograft rejection (151,116) follow, reflecting immune biases from lymphoid samples. Rare pathways like hallmark spermatogenesis (3,109) are underrepresented, possibly due to tissue specificity.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad/full_2026_07_06_rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad_docling_json_20260812_200246/docling/document.json:6227:      "text": "Figure 8 shows a skewed distribution, with hallmark TNFα via NF κ B (302,664) and hallmark UV response DN (205,134) leading, likely due to inflammation and stress studies, boosted by brain and blood tissue dominance (Figure 6). Pathways like hallmark interferonα response (167,957) and hallmark allograft rejection (151,116) follow, reflecting immune biases from lymphoid samples. Rare pathways like hallmark spermatogenesis (3,109) are underrepresented, possibly due to tissue specificity."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6/full_2026_07_06_rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6_docling_json_20260812_193428/docling/chunks.json:2459:    "text": "4. Experiments\n4.5. Interpretability Analysis\nWe probe model interpretability with Grad-CAM on the multi-page fusion representations (see Appendix K.7 for implementation details). Fig. 4 visualizes two pages from a donor-labeled Japonica test document. Unlike sequencebased models that distribute attention diffusely at the base level, OpticalDNA produces localized attributions over short contiguous regions, reflecting region-level processing induced by visual tokenization and local receptive fields. This phrase-like reading behavior mirrors how humans process long text and is particularly well suited for long DNA documents, as it avoids exhaustive base-wise scanning while preserving informative local patterns under a fixed token budget. Importantly, Grad-CAM activations concentrate around the annotated donor splice site (purple box), and the corresponding page receives higher overall attribution, indicating that OpticalDNA can both localize biologically meaningful splice signals and prioritize the most relevant pages during inference. Overall, these results indicate that OpticalDNA learns structured, region-aware representations aligned with genomic reasoning, while also offering practical interpretability, highlighting its potential utility for large-scale genomic and transcriptomic analyses.\nTable 6. ROI identification on T2 (IoU = 0 . 99 ).\n\nOpticalDNA-HG38, LCM ↑ = 0.9871. OpticalDNA-HG38, Joint ↑ = 0.9820. OpticalDNA-HG38, Strict ↑ = 0.8763. OpticalDNA-HG38, ℓ ∞ ↓ = 0.3941. OpticalDNA-Rice, LCM ↑ = 0.9991. OpticalDNA-Rice, Joint ↑ = 0.9692. OpticalDNA-Rice, Strict ↑ = 0.8084. OpticalDNA-Rice, ℓ ∞ ↓ = 0.3243\nAbbrev. LCM: line-count match; Joint: line-level joint accuracy (text+ROI); Strict: page-level strict success.\nFigure 4. Grad-CAM visualization on multi-page fusion for a donor case (two pages). Purple boxes indicate donor splice sites; numbers denote page-level mean attribution.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows two side-by-side text panels containing dense nucleotide/DNA-like sequences highlighted in green. Each panel appears to be a page or sequence block comparison view.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Left panel labeled `Page#0: 0.530`\n- Right panel labeled `Page#4: 0.585`\n- A horizontal color/score bar at the bottom ranging from `0` to `1`, with midpoint `0.5`\n- Most sequence text is shaded green, suggesting a continuous heatmap or confidence/similarity overlay\n- A vertical divider separates the two panels\n\nBiological source objects:\n- DNA/RNA nucleotide strings composed primarily of A, C, G, and T characters\n\nLikely transformation/interface:\n- Sequence text has been rendered as page images and overlaid with a green intensity map, possibly representing similarity, attribution, relevance, or matching score.\n- The page-level numeric labels may indicate scores for each extracted page/panel.\n\nVisible finding:\n- Both panels contain strong green-highlighted sequence regions, with the right panel having a slightly higher displayed score (`0.585`) than the left (`0.530`).",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6/full_2026_07_06_rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6_docling_json_20260812_193428/docling/chunks.json:2691:    "text": "4. Experiments\n4.7. Rendering Robustness\nFigure 6. Low-resolution robustness on the Adipose Subcutaneous eQTL task. AUROC remains stable under moderate downsampling and degrades mainly at extreme resolutions.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle line chart showing model performance across image downsampling resolutions.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: “Downsampled resolution” with labels `64×64`, `128×128`, `256×256`, `320×320`, `640×640`.\n- Y-axis: “AUROC”, ranging roughly from `0.76` to `0.84`.\n- Green line with circular markers connects AUROC values:\n  - `64×64`: `0.768`\n  - `128×128`: `0.799`\n  - `256×256`: `0.812`\n  - `320×320`: `0.828`\n  - `640×640`: `0.813`\n- Dashed horizontal reference line labeled “Native 640×640” near AUROC `0.812`.\n- Finding: AUROC improves from low resolution to `320×320`, peaks at `0.828`, then decreases slightly at `640×640` to `0.813`, close to the native-resolution reference line.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, anatomical structures, transformations, or model interface diagrams are visible in this extracted image.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000133_3abbdd0df15f/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000133_3abbdd0df15f_docling_json_20260812_220833/docling/chunks.json:5863:    "text": "GenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nResults\nBroad and meaningful sequence generation\nOne  of  the  central  goals  of  GenNA  is  to  enable  conditional  nucleotide  generation  guided  by natural-language annotations. To evaluate whether the generated outputs are not only formally plausible but  also  consistent  with  the  underlying  biological  context  in  both  semantic  and  statistical  terms,  we systematically analyzed model behavior across multiple generation settings (Fig. 3a).\nWe first evaluated unconditional generation under highly minimal conditions. In this setting, all explicit constraints were removed, and the model was provided only with a modality tag (Genomic DNA or RNA). It was then required to autoregressively generate the complete metadata fields, including species, gene name, and functional  annotation,  followed  by  the  corresponding  nucleotide  sequence.  For  generated mRNA sequences, we extracted the CDS regions and translated them into protein sequences, which were then aligned against existing databases using BLASTP 33 . We subsequently used Sentence-Transformers 34 to evaluate the semantic consistency between the model-generated functional annotations and the annotations associated with the top database hits. Sequence-logo analysis of these unconditionally generated mRNAs further recovered a Kozak-like translation-initiation context, which is a meaningful  local  sequence  grammar  (Supplementary Fig.  5).  The  results  showed  that  the  generated annotations remained well aligned with the corresponding generated sequences at the semantic level (Fig. 3b).\nWe  next  evaluated  species-specific  generation  to  investigate  whether  the  model  could  leverage species-specific  properties  to guide sequence  generation. Without  imposing  any  explicit constraint  on gene function, we prompted the model to generate nucleotide sequences from six representative model organisms:  human (Homo  sapiens) ,  zebrafish (Danio  rerio) , fruit fly (Drosophila  melanogaster) , Arabidopsis  thaliana ,  yeast (Saccharomyces  cerevisiae) ,  and  maize (Zea  mays) . In addition to preserving semantic self-consistency (Fig. 3c), GenNA successfully reproduced species-specific features in  the  generated  sequences  (Fig.  3d-g).  For  example,  the  GC-content  distributions  of  the  generated sequences were broadly consistent with those of the corresponding reference genomes, and the model captured systematic differences across species ranging from relatively low-GC to high-GC backgrounds. Similar trends were also observed for GC3, codon-usage bias, and amino acid frequency.\nIn addition, we observed that the generated distributions were more concentrated than the corresponding natural reference distributions, exhibiting a degree of distribution sharpening. In fruit fly and maize, for example, the generated sequences tended to cluster more strongly around the most representative GC ranges  of  each  species,  whereas  coverage  of  the  long-tail  regions  in  the  natural  distributions  was relatively limited (Fig. 3e). This pattern suggests that, under the current sampling configuration, GenNA preferentially generates typical samples that conform to the dominant modes of the training distribution\nwhile still underrepresenting low-frequency tail variation 35,36 . Overall, these results indicate that GenNA\n239\n240\n241\n242\n243\n244\n245\n246\n247\n248\n249\n250\n251\n252\n253\n254\n255\n256\n257\n258\n259\n260\n261\n262\n263\n264\n265\n266\n267\n268\n269\ncan generate sequences that are not only consistent with its self-generated functional annotations but also preserve species-specific statistical features.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002517_46d135b6aad4/full_2026_07_06_rec_002517_46d135b6aad4_docling_json_20260812_213057/docling/document.dclg:1293:<![CDATA[Vocabulary adaptation improves domainspecific language understanding. To further investigate vocabulary adaptation's effect, we use t-SNE to visualize general versus domain-specific word embeddings before and after training, shown in Figure 4. Initially, embeddings overlap significantly. After training, domain-specific words begin to form discrete clusters, evidenced]]>  </text>
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002517_46d135b6aad4/full_2026_07_06_rec_002517_46d135b6aad4_docling_json_20260812_213057/docling/document.dclg:1312:      Figure 4: Visualization of word embeddings before and after training. A lower DB index indicates better clustering separation.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002517_46d135b6aad4/full_2026_07_06_rec_002517_46d135b6aad4_docling_json_20260812_213057/docling/document.dclg:1411:<![CDATA[CHATCELL accurately captures cell type characteristics in generation. To assess the biological accuracy of cell sentences generated by CHATCELL, we visualize their corresponding gene expression matrices in two dimensions, following the pseudo-cell generation experimental setting. Figure 6 (a) shows that cell distributions generated by CHATCELL closely resemble those of real cells, confirmed by Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD). The distinct clustering of cell types in the generated data demonstrates CHATCELL's ability to capture and differentiate unique cell characteristics, indicating its capacity to generate detailed profiles for each cell type.]]>  </text>
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002517_46d135b6aad4/full_2026_07_06_rec_002517_46d135b6aad4_docling_json_20260812_213057/docling/document.dclg:1418:    CHATCELL uncovers gene-level biological insights. Each cell sentence is composed of genes. To delve into the biological significance of the representations learned at a finer granularity, we analyze gene associations based on their embeddings obtained by CHATCELL. We employ K -means clustering to categorize genes, grouping those with high associations, as depicted in Figure 6 (b). For each cluster, we further conduct gene pathway enrichment analysis (Aleksander et al., 2023). This analysis, which focuses on a series of interconnected biochemical reactions working together to perform specific biological functions like energy production or cell growth, offers insights into the biological processes each cluster is involved in. Specifically, we map these genes to their related pathways in the Gene Ontology (Ashburner et al., 2000) to identify the biological pathways signifi-
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002517_46d135b6aad4/full_2026_07_06_rec_002517_46d135b6aad4_docling_json_20260812_213057/docling/document.dclg:1437:      Figure 6: (a) Distribution of real cells and generated cells. (b) Gene clusters and their associated pathways.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002517_46d135b6aad4/full_2026_07_06_rec_002517_46d135b6aad4_docling_json_20260812_213057/docling/document.dclg:2240:    Meanwhile, Figure 8 provides a detailed expansion of Figure 6 (b), showing each gene cluster and
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002517_46d135b6aad4/full_2026_07_06_rec_002517_46d135b6aad4_docling_json_20260812_213057/docling/document.md:264:Vocabulary adaptation improves domainspecific language understanding. To further investigate vocabulary adaptation's effect, we use t-SNE to visualize general versus domain-specific word embeddings before and after training, shown in Figure 4. Initially, embeddings overlap significantly. After training, domain-specific words begin to form discrete clusters, evidenced by a lower Davies-Bouldin (DB) index, indicating better clustering. This implies that the embeddings of these words have become more representative of their specific meanings and contexts within their respective domains, rather than remaining ambiguously intermingled with general words. In contrast, general word embeddings remain largely unchanged, highlighting the precision of our vocabulary adaptation strategy in bolstering domain expertise without compromising general language processing.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002517_46d135b6aad4/full_2026_07_06_rec_002517_46d135b6aad4_docling_json_20260812_213057/docling/document.md:266:Figure 4: Visualization of word embeddings before and after training. A lower DB index indicates better clustering separation.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002517_46d135b6aad4/full_2026_07_06_rec_002517_46d135b6aad4_docling_json_20260812_213057/docling/document.md:336:CHATCELL accurately captures cell type characteristics in generation. To assess the biological accuracy of cell sentences generated by CHATCELL, we visualize their corresponding gene expression matrices in two dimensions, following the pseudo-cell generation experimental setting. Figure 6 (a) shows that cell distributions generated by CHATCELL closely resemble those of real cells, confirmed by Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD). The distinct clustering of cell types in the generated data demonstrates CHATCELL's ability to capture and differentiate unique cell characteristics, indicating its capacity to generate detailed profiles for each cell type.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002517_46d135b6aad4/full_2026_07_06_rec_002517_46d135b6aad4_docling_json_20260812_213057/docling/document.md:338:CHATCELL uncovers gene-level biological insights. Each cell sentence is composed of genes. To delve into the biological significance of the representations learned at a finer granularity, we analyze gene associations based on their embeddings obtained by CHATCELL. We employ K -means clustering to categorize genes, grouping those with high associations, as depicted in Figure 6 (b). For each cluster, we further conduct gene pathway enrichment analysis (Aleksander et al., 2023). This analysis, which focuses on a series of interconnected biochemical reactions working together to perform specific biological functions like energy production or cell growth, offers insights into the biological processes each cluster is involved in. Specifically, we map these genes to their related pathways in the Gene Ontology (Ashburner et al., 2000) to identify the biological pathways signifi- cantly associated with them, with the significance of these associations quantified by p -values, as illustrated in Appendix Figure 8. As illustrated, genes in cluster 1 are linked to tissue structure regulation, while those in cluster 2 are predominantly related to the immune system, inflammatory responses, and cell signaling. Cluster 3 encompasses genes associated with immune cell activation and signaling, cluster 4 with leukocyte functions and immune responses, and cluster 5 primarily with tissue development. The distinct separation of these gene clusters further demonstrates CHATCELL's effective capture of intricate relationships between genes and their functions, validating its profound understanding of complex biological processes.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002517_46d135b6aad4/full_2026_07_06_rec_002517_46d135b6aad4_docling_json_20260812_213057/docling/document.md:340:Figure 6: (a) Distribution of real cells and generated cells. (b) Gene clusters and their associated pathways.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002517_46d135b6aad4/full_2026_07_06_rec_002517_46d135b6aad4_docling_json_20260812_213057/docling/document.md:554:Meanwhile, Figure 8 provides a detailed expansion of Figure 6 (b), showing each gene cluster and its most associated pathways. This proves CHATCELL's ability to capture the relationships between genes and their functions, thereby enhancing its comprehension of biological processes.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001343_6968c1123911/full_2026_07_06_rec_001343_6968c1123911_docling_json_20260812_194019/docling_graph/provenance.json:4038:      "text": "B FURTHER DETAILS OF scDiff\nB.1 SINUSOIDAL TIME EMBEDDING\nIn equation 10, we implement sinusoidal time embedding as:\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\nFigure 4: Illustration of context conditioner.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA schematic model diagram showing information flow through layered computation and auxiliary error/objective terms.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Left vertical stack: “context” input flows upward through “Layer 1”, “Layer 2”, an ellipsis, and “Layer L”.\n- Right vertical stack: top input labeled `x_t` with downward arrow and parameter/conditioning label `φ`, feeding into `𝓔^(1)`.\n- The right-side modules continue downward through an ellipsis, `𝓔^(L−1)`, `𝓔^(L)`, then `𝓓`, ending at output `x_θ`.\n- Dashed horizontal arrows connect each left layer to corresponding right-side error/energy modules: Layer 1 to `𝓔^(L)`, Layer 2 to `𝓔^(L−1)`, Layer L to `𝓔^(1)`.\n- Solid arrows indicate sequential vertical flow within both the layer stack and the right-side module stack.\n\nBiological source objects: none visible.\n\nModel/interface content:\n- Depicts a layered neural/model architecture or inference/denoising-style pipeline linking context representations to error/energy terms `𝓔` and decoder/decision component `𝓓`.\n- No quantitative results or empirical findings are shown.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001343_6968c1123911/full_2026_07_06_rec_001343_6968c1123911_docling_json_20260812_194019/docling_graph/provenance.json:5173:      "text": "E EXPERIMENTAL SETTINGS AND RESULTS\nE.4 FEW-SHOT CELL TYPE ANNOTATION\nWe summarize the macro accuracy score of few-shot cell type annotation in Fig. 6 and macro F1 score in Fig. 7. We fix the number of target cell type to 5 . In Brain and Immune, LLM-guided scDiff shows significant performance gain against class-guided scDiff . In the other two datasets, the two variants of scDiff achieve comaprable performance.\nFigure 6: Macro accuracy score of few-shot cell type annotation on top 5 cell types.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel line plot comparing ACC performance across datasets or biological domains.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Brain\n- Liver\n- HLCA\n- Immune\n\nAxes and labels:\n- Shared x-axis label: “Number of cells per type”\n- Y-axis label: “ACC”\n- X-axis ticks appear at 0, 5, and 10.\n- Y-axis values shown around 0.6 and 0.8.\n\nLegend/model interfaces:\n- Blue line: “scDiff-Class”\n- Orange line: “scDiff-LLM”\n- Shaded bands around lines indicate variability or uncertainty, but the exact statistic is not specified.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Brain cells\n- Liver cells\n- HLCA cells, likely Human Lung Cell Atlas context based on acronym, though the figure only labels it “HLCA”\n- Immune cells\n\nFindings visible:\n- scDiff-LLM generally performs as well as or better than scDiff-Class across panels.\n- In Brain, scDiff-LLM rises quickly and remains above scDiff-Class for most cell counts.\n- In Liver, both methods achieve high ACC, with very similar curves.\n- In HLCA, both methods are close, with small fluctuations and no large separation.\n- In Immune, scDiff-LLM is consistently higher than scDiff-Class, especially at lower cell counts.\nFigure 7: Macro F1 score of few-shot cell type annotation on top 5 cell types.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel line plot comparing F1 score performance across different biological datasets or tissue/source categories.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Brain\n- Liver\n- HLCA\n- Immune\n\nAxes and labels:\n- Y-axis: `F1 score`\n- X-axis: `Number of cells per type`\n- X-axis values range approximately from 0 to 10.\n- F1 scores range roughly from 0.5 to above 0.9 depending on panel.\n\nLegend / model interfaces:\n- Blue line: `scDiff-Class`\n- Orange line: `scDiff-LLM`\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Cell-type classification or prediction appears to be evaluated across datasets/sources labeled Brain, Liver, HLCA, and Immune.\n- The x-axis indicates varying numbers of cells per type.\n\nTransformations / visual encoding:\n- Four small-multiple line charts.\n- Performance is plotted as F1 score versus number of cells per type.\n- Two model methods are compared using colored lines.\n\nVisible findings:\n- `scDiff-LLM` generally matches or exceeds `scDiff-Class` across most panels.\n- Liver shows high F1 scores for both methods after a small number of cells per type, approaching about 0.95.\n- Brain has lower and more variable F1 scores than Liver.\n- HLCA shows both methods clustered around the high 0.8 range after several cells per type.\n- Immune shows increasing performance with more cells per type, with `scDiff-LLM` generally above `scDiff-Class`.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000133_3abbdd0df15f/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000133_3abbdd0df15f_docling_json_20260812_220833/docling/document.md:521:We  next  evaluated  species-specific  generation  to  investigate  whether  the  model  could  leverage species-specific  properties  to guide sequence  generation. Without  imposing  any  explicit constraint  on gene function, we prompted the model to generate nucleotide sequences from six representative model organisms:  human (Homo  sapiens) ,  zebrafish (Danio  rerio) , fruit fly (Drosophila  melanogaster) , Arabidopsis  thaliana ,  yeast (Saccharomyces  cerevisiae) ,  and  maize (Zea  mays) . In addition to preserving semantic self-consistency (Fig. 3c), GenNA successfully reproduced species-specific features in  the  generated  sequences  (Fig.  3d-g).  For  example,  the  GC-content  distributions  of  the  generated sequences were broadly consistent with those of the corresponding reference genomes, and the model captured systematic differences across species ranging from relatively low-GC to high-GC backgrounds. Similar trends were also observed for GC3, codon-usage bias, and amino acid frequency.
