./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6/full_2026_07_06_rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6_docling_json_20260812_193428/docling/document.md:282:Figure 4. Grad-CAM visualization on multi-page fusion for a donor case (two pages). Purple boxes indicate donor splice sites; numbers denote page-level mean attribution.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6/full_2026_07_06_rec_001830_eb50f2bf58e6_docling_json_20260812_193428/docling/document.md:360:Figure 6. Low-resolution robustness on the Adipose Subcutaneous eQTL task. AUROC remains stable under moderate downsampling and degrades mainly at extreme resolutions.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001343_6968c1123911/full_2026_07_06_rec_001343_6968c1123911_docling_json_20260812_194019/docling/chunks.json:4025:    "text": "B FURTHER DETAILS OF scDiff\nB.1 SINUSOIDAL TIME EMBEDDING\nIn equation 10, we implement sinusoidal time embedding as:\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\nFigure 4: Illustration of context conditioner.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA schematic model diagram showing information flow through layered computation and auxiliary error/objective terms.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Left vertical stack: “context” input flows upward through “Layer 1”, “Layer 2”, an ellipsis, and “Layer L”.\n- Right vertical stack: top input labeled `x_t` with downward arrow and parameter/conditioning label `φ`, feeding into `𝓔^(1)`.\n- The right-side modules continue downward through an ellipsis, `𝓔^(L−1)`, `𝓔^(L)`, then `𝓓`, ending at output `x_θ`.\n- Dashed horizontal arrows connect each left layer to corresponding right-side error/energy modules: Layer 1 to `𝓔^(L)`, Layer 2 to `𝓔^(L−1)`, Layer L to `𝓔^(1)`.\n- Solid arrows indicate sequential vertical flow within both the layer stack and the right-side module stack.\n\nBiological source objects: none visible.\n\nModel/interface content:\n- Depicts a layered neural/model architecture or inference/denoising-style pipeline linking context representations to error/energy terms `𝓔` and decoder/decision component `𝓓`.\n- No quantitative results or empirical findings are shown.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001343_6968c1123911/full_2026_07_06_rec_001343_6968c1123911_docling_json_20260812_194019/docling/chunks.json:5160:    "text": "E EXPERIMENTAL SETTINGS AND RESULTS\nE.4 FEW-SHOT CELL TYPE ANNOTATION\nWe summarize the macro accuracy score of few-shot cell type annotation in Fig. 6 and macro F1 score in Fig. 7. We fix the number of target cell type to 5 . In Brain and Immune, LLM-guided scDiff shows significant performance gain against class-guided scDiff . In the other two datasets, the two variants of scDiff achieve comaprable performance.\nFigure 6: Macro accuracy score of few-shot cell type annotation on top 5 cell types.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel line plot comparing ACC performance across datasets or biological domains.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Brain\n- Liver\n- HLCA\n- Immune\n\nAxes and labels:\n- Shared x-axis label: “Number of cells per type”\n- Y-axis label: “ACC”\n- X-axis ticks appear at 0, 5, and 10.\n- Y-axis values shown around 0.6 and 0.8.\n\nLegend/model interfaces:\n- Blue line: “scDiff-Class”\n- Orange line: “scDiff-LLM”\n- Shaded bands around lines indicate variability or uncertainty, but the exact statistic is not specified.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Brain cells\n- Liver cells\n- HLCA cells, likely Human Lung Cell Atlas context based on acronym, though the figure only labels it “HLCA”\n- Immune cells\n\nFindings visible:\n- scDiff-LLM generally performs as well as or better than scDiff-Class across panels.\n- In Brain, scDiff-LLM rises quickly and remains above scDiff-Class for most cell counts.\n- In Liver, both methods achieve high ACC, with very similar curves.\n- In HLCA, both methods are close, with small fluctuations and no large separation.\n- In Immune, scDiff-LLM is consistently higher than scDiff-Class, especially at lower cell counts.\nFigure 7: Macro F1 score of few-shot cell type annotation on top 5 cell types.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel line plot comparing F1 score performance across different biological datasets or tissue/source categories.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Brain\n- Liver\n- HLCA\n- Immune\n\nAxes and labels:\n- Y-axis: `F1 score`\n- X-axis: `Number of cells per type`\n- X-axis values range approximately from 0 to 10.\n- F1 scores range roughly from 0.5 to above 0.9 depending on panel.\n\nLegend / model interfaces:\n- Blue line: `scDiff-Class`\n- Orange line: `scDiff-LLM`\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Cell-type classification or prediction appears to be evaluated across datasets/sources labeled Brain, Liver, HLCA, and Immune.\n- The x-axis indicates varying numbers of cells per type.\n\nTransformations / visual encoding:\n- Four small-multiple line charts.\n- Performance is plotted as F1 score versus number of cells per type.\n- Two model methods are compared using colored lines.\n\nVisible findings:\n- `scDiff-LLM` generally matches or exceeds `scDiff-Class` across most panels.\n- Liver shows high F1 scores for both methods after a small number of cells per type, approaching about 0.95.\n- Brain has lower and more variable F1 scores than Liver.\n- HLCA shows both methods clustered around the high 0.8 range after several cells per type.\n- Immune shows increasing performance with more cells per type, with `scDiff-LLM` generally above `scDiff-Class`.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000148_32d2df447629/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000148_32d2df447629_docling_json_20260812_203724/debug/trace_data.json:67:            "text_content": "Figure 4: Spot-level spatial domain recognition in the human DLPFC. a , Schematic illustration of spot-level spatial domain recognition. For each spot, SciCore-Omics takes the matched histology image patch and/or gene expression profile as input and generates a description, from which region labels, pathway labels and marker genes are extracted. b , Test-set macro-F1 scores for brain-region label prediction using linear-probing models computed from frozen embeddings generated by SciCoreOmics, Nicheformer, Geneformer, scGPT, scVI and PCA. c , Accuracy of region, pathway and markergene prediction for models after fine-tuning. Bubble size and colour indicate ACC for each structured readout. Grey crosses indicate unsupported input-output settings. d , Spatial projection of manual and predicted brain-region labels in held-out DLPFC sections. e , UMAP visualization of image-only, geneonly and joint image-gene embeddings coloured by Glutamatergic synapse pathway activity.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about a multimodal omics/image model named **SciCore-Omics**.\n\nVisible content:\n\n- **Panel a:** Schematic model interface. Inputs are an **image patch** showing stained tissue morphology and **gene expression** shown as a small bar chart. These feed into **SciCore-Omics**, which produces a natural-language description and structured readout extraction with three output fields:\n  - **region label**\n  - **pathway label**\n  - **marker genes**\n\n- **Panel b:** Bar chart comparing **F1 macro** performance for **Brain region label** prediction. Methods include SciCore-Omics variants:\n  - image embedding\n  - gene embedding\n  - joint embedding  \n  and baselines including **Nicheformer**, **Geneformer**, **ScGPT**, **ScVI**, and **PCA** variants. SciCore-Omics joint embedding appears highest, with image embedding also high.\n\n- **Panel c:** Bubble-matrix accuracy comparison. Rows include **Scicore-Omics**, **LLaVA-Med**, **BioMedGPT**, **CellWhisperer... [truncated, total 3258 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000148_32d2df447629/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000148_32d2df447629_docling_json_20260812_203724/debug/trace_data.json:82:            "text_content": "Figure 6: H&amp;E-based pathology reasoning with SciCore-Omics. a , Fine-tuned PathVQA performance on open-ended and closed-ended questions compared with biomedical VLM baselines. b , H&amp;Eonly case-level reasoning workflow, from low-power slide review to high-power patch assessment and case-level synthesis; Ki-67 immunohistochemistry and pathology reports were used only for expert evaluation. c , Expert assessment of SciCore-Omics, Qwen3-VL and LLaVA-Med on 10 breast cancer cases across six reasoning dimensions. d , Representative proliferation-state inference from tumour-rich regions, high-power morphology and aggregated case-level evidence.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical AI/pathology figure about H&E slide-based tumor assessment and case-level evidence synthesis.\n\nPanel **a**: Table comparing methods with **Open** and **Closed** scores. Methods shown: SciCore-Omics, MMQ, M2I2, BioMedGPT, LLaVA-Med. SciCore-Omics has Open **38.10** and Closed **88.66**; LLaVA-Med has Open **37.95** and Closed **91.21** highlighted.\n\nPanel **b**: Workflow diagram. Input is **H&E only**, shown as a whole-slide histology image. The process includes **low-magnification overview**, **tumor patch selection**, and **high-magnification morphology reasoning**. Some candidate patches are marked with red Xs, while selected histology patches proceed to feature reasoning. Visible morphology labels include **cellularity**, **nuclear atypia**, **hyperchromasia**, **suspected mitoses**, **necrosis**, and **stromal reaction**. Evidence from patches is aggregated into an **evidence summary** with high/low/uncertain indicators, supporting **case-level evidence synthesis**. Pathologist evaluation is shown using an independent reference: **Ki-67 IHC + pathology report**.\n\nPanel **c**: Radar/spider plot comparing **SciCore-Omics**, **qwen3vl**, and **LLaVA-Med** across evaluation dimensions: **Accuracy**, **Balanced accuracy**, **Reasoning completeness**, **Evidence qualit... [truncated, total 3395 chars]",
