./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/update_2026-08-09__rec_000138_796e32b2faec/update_2026_08_09_rec_000138_796e32b2faec_docling_json_20260812_205735/docling_graph/graph.html:526:        "supporting_figure_or_table": "Figure 4b-d",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_213830/docling/document.json:2837:      "orig": "To gain a deeper understanding of OmniNA's capabilities in aligning sequence and textual patterns with natural lan- guage, we investigated the token representations of the pretrained OmniNA-1.7B (see the 'Materials and methods' section). The t-SNE plot reveals the model's ability to discriminate tokens representing distinct semantics, such as genus, genes, and sequences (Fig. 3 A). Besides, tokens for gene symbols and gene functions exhibit distinct clustering, while sequence tokens with different base preferences manifest in separate loci (Fig. 3 A). Moreover, taxonomy names are distinctly separated, with tokens from the same taxonomy clustering together (Fig. 3 B). We also observed that viruses from different phyla form distinct subclusters (Fig. 3 B).",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_213830/docling/document.json:2838:      "text": "To gain a deeper understanding of OmniNA's capabilities in aligning sequence and textual patterns with natural lan- guage, we investigated the token representations of the pretrained OmniNA-1.7B (see the 'Materials and methods' section). The t-SNE plot reveals the model's ability to discriminate tokens representing distinct semantics, such as genus, genes, and sequences (Fig. 3 A). Besides, tokens for gene symbols and gene functions exhibit distinct clustering, while sequence tokens with different base preferences manifest in separate loci (Fig. 3 A). Moreover, taxonomy names are distinctly separated, with tokens from the same taxonomy clustering together (Fig. 3 B). We also observed that viruses from different phyla form distinct subclusters (Fig. 3 B)."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_213830/docling/document.json:2999:      "orig": "Figure 2. Evaluation of OmniNA's generation performance. ( A ) Fidelity assessment in sequence annotations generated by OmniNA models. For each target and model, the precision, recall, and F1 score represent the mean score across all labels. ( B ) Example of generated annotations for the same input by OmniNA models of varying sizes. ( C ) (Left) Bar plot illustrating Shannon entropy for taxonomy token generation. The heatmap at the bottom represents the generated token numbers. (Right) Line chart depicting generated token numbers correlated with token frequency. ( D ) (Left) Bar plot illustrating Shannon entrop y f or gene function token generation. The heatmap at the bottom represents the generated token numbers. (Right) Line chart depicting generated token numbers related to token frequency. ( E ) Box plot showcasing the cosine similarity of feature representations among different annotations generated from the same sentence. ( F ) t-SNE plot deciphering feature representations of generated annotations, with annotations from the same sequence colored identically. ( G ) B o x plot reflecting the cosine similarity of token embeddings among different annotations generated from different truncations of the same sequence. ( H ) t-SNE plot deciphering the feature representations of generated annotations, with annotations from the same genome colored identically. ∗∗∗ P -value < 0.001; ∗∗ P -value < 0.01; ∗ P -value < 0.05.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_213830/docling/document.json:3000:      "text": "Figure 2. Evaluation of OmniNA's generation performance. ( A ) Fidelity assessment in sequence annotations generated by OmniNA models. For each target and model, the precision, recall, and F1 score represent the mean score across all labels. ( B ) Example of generated annotations for the same input by OmniNA models of varying sizes. ( C ) (Left) Bar plot illustrating Shannon entropy for taxonomy token generation. The heatmap at the bottom represents the generated token numbers. (Right) Line chart depicting generated token numbers correlated with token frequency. ( D ) (Left) Bar plot illustrating Shannon entrop y f or gene function token generation. The heatmap at the bottom represents the generated token numbers. (Right) Line chart depicting generated token numbers related to token frequency. ( E ) Box plot showcasing the cosine similarity of feature representations among different annotations generated from the same sentence. ( F ) t-SNE plot deciphering feature representations of generated annotations, with annotations from the same sequence colored identically. ( G ) B o x plot reflecting the cosine similarity of token embeddings among different annotations generated from different truncations of the same sequence. ( H ) t-SNE plot deciphering the feature representations of generated annotations, with annotations from the same genome colored identically. ∗∗∗ P -value < 0.001; ∗∗ P -value < 0.01; ∗ P -value < 0.05."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_213830/docling/document.json:3408:      "orig": "Figure 4. Performance of fine-tuned OmniNA on nucleotide sequence identification tasks. ( A ) Radar plot shows the F1 score achieved by OmniNA and the benchmark method on the task of DNA element detection. Bar plot illustrating the F1 score achie v ed b y OmniNA model and the benchmark method on the task of splice site detection ( B ), PAS/TIS site detection ( C ), and gRNA off-target prediction ( D ). ( E ) Dot plot depicts the performance of OmniNA with different parameters on the task of methylated region detection. The circle size reflects the precision score of method, and the color reflects the recall of the method. The bar plot in the above panel shows the F1 score of the OmniNA model with different parameters. ( F ) The performance of OmniNA and three benchmark methods, i.e. Enformer, Sei, and ExPecto, on chromatin feature detection tasks. ( G ) The boxplot presents the median, upper, and lo w er quartiles of the F1 score for the pathogenic variation detection performance across all genes. ( H ) Radar plot shows the F1 score of the OmniNA-1.7B model and benchmark method for pathogenic SNV detection. ( I ) Violin and box plots comparing the o v erall F1 score distributions between EVE and OmniNA-1.7B. T -test was applied for the analysis. ( J ) Bar plot illustrating the F1 score achieved by OmniNA and the benchmark method on the task of pathogenic virus detection. Data are randomly sampled 1000 times from each branch, with 10 0 0 strains per sample. The center line represents the mean, and the error bar indicates the 95% CI. ( K ) Bar plot illustrating the F1 score achieved by OmniNA and the benchmark method on the task of bacteria species classification. Data are randomly sampled 1000 times from each branch, with 10 0 0 strains per sample. The center line represents the mean, and the error bar indicates the 95% CI. ( L ) The F1 score comparison between the OmniNA-1.7B model and benchmark method on bacteria phylum classification task. ∗∗∗ P -value < 0.001.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_213830/docling/document.json:3409:      "text": "Figure 4. Performance of fine-tuned OmniNA on nucleotide sequence identification tasks. ( A ) Radar plot shows the F1 score achieved by OmniNA and the benchmark method on the task of DNA element detection. Bar plot illustrating the F1 score achie v ed b y OmniNA model and the benchmark method on the task of splice site detection ( B ), PAS/TIS site detection ( C ), and gRNA off-target prediction ( D ). ( E ) Dot plot depicts the performance of OmniNA with different parameters on the task of methylated region detection. The circle size reflects the precision score of method, and the color reflects the recall of the method. The bar plot in the above panel shows the F1 score of the OmniNA model with different parameters. ( F ) The performance of OmniNA and three benchmark methods, i.e. Enformer, Sei, and ExPecto, on chromatin feature detection tasks. ( G ) The boxplot presents the median, upper, and lo w er quartiles of the F1 score for the pathogenic variation detection performance across all genes. ( H ) Radar plot shows the F1 score of the OmniNA-1.7B model and benchmark method for pathogenic SNV detection. ( I ) Violin and box plots comparing the o v erall F1 score distributions between EVE and OmniNA-1.7B. T -test was applied for the analysis. ( J ) Bar plot illustrating the F1 score achieved by OmniNA and the benchmark method on the task of pathogenic virus detection. Data are randomly sampled 1000 times from each branch, with 10 0 0 strains per sample. The center line represents the mean, and the error bar indicates the 95% CI. ( K ) Bar plot illustrating the F1 score achieved by OmniNA and the benchmark method on the task of bacteria species classification. Data are randomly sampled 1000 times from each branch, with 10 0 0 strains per sample. The center line represents the mean, and the error bar indicates the 95% CI. ( L ) The F1 score comparison between the OmniNA-1.7B model and benchmark method on bacteria phylum classification task. ∗∗∗ P -value < 0.001."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_213830/docling/document.json:6069:          "text": "SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled A-H comparing OmniNA language models on nucleotide-related tasks.\n\nVisible content:\n- Panel A: Performance table for three tasks: nucleotide sequence classification, taxonomy classification, and enzyme classification. Models compared are OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, and OmniNA-66M. Metrics shown are Precision, Recall, and F1, with blue/green heatmap shading.\n- Panel B: Example nucleotide input sequence and generated model responses. Shows a true response for an Amolops voucher KIZ014116 tyrosinase gene, partial CDS, and outputs from OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, and OmniNA-66M. Entity spans are color-highlighted, including organism names, voucher/accession-like identifiers, gene/protein terms, CDS, mRNA, and “uncharacterized protein.”\n- Panel C: Taxonomy token statistics. Bar chart reports Shannon entropy and token number for OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, and OmniNA-66M. Adjacent line plot shows token number across token-frequency bins.\n- Panel D: Gene function token statistics. Similar Shannon entropy/token number bar chart and token-frequency distribution line plot for the three OmniNA models.\n- Panel E: Boxplots of cosine similarity for OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, OmniNA-66M, and Random, with significance markers.\n- Panel F: Fit-SNE embedding plot showing clustered sequence/function representations. Annotated clusters include “mRNA of dehydrogenase,” “mRNA of oxidase,” “Chloroplast CDS/mRNA,” and “Subunit of NSL complex.” At right, an example nucleotide input and repeated OmniNA-1.7B responses list multiple species names paired with “KAT8 regulatory NSL complex subunit 2.”\n- Panel G: Boxplots of cosine similarity for HIV gene-related outputs from OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, OmniNA-66M, and Random, with significance markers.\n- Panel H: Fit-SNE embedding of HIV1 gene representations. Legend includes HIV1 genes: gag, gag-pol, vif, vpr, vpu, env, and nef. A schematic genome-like gene map is shown above/right, and model response examples list HIV-1 isolate descriptions with highlighted gene labels such as gag and pol.\n\nBiological source objects and concepts visible:\n- Nucleotide sequences.\n- Taxonomic labels/organism names.\n- Genes and gene products, including tyrosinase, KAT8 regulatory NSL complex subunit 2, HIV1 gag, gag-pol, vif, vpr, vpu, env, and nef.\n- Sequence annotation terms such as CDS, mRNA, partial CDS, and protein.\n\nModel/interface details:\n- Evaluates OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, and OmniNA-66M.\n- Shows prompt-like nucleotide “Input” strings and text “Response” outputs.\n- Uses highlighted spans to compare generated annotations against true responses.\n- Uses tokenization/statistical analyses, cosine similarity boxplots, and Fit-SNE embeddings to characterize model outputs and learned representations.\n\nMain visible findings:\n- Larger OmniNA models generally show higher classification scores and higher cosine similarity than smaller models and random baseline.\n- Token statistics differ by model size for taxonomy and gene function.\n- Embeddings cluster by biological annotation/function categories, including chloroplast annotations and HIV1 gene classes."
